t clean speech spectra [1].\nAlternatively, DNNs can be trained to estimate time-frequency\nmasks, i.e., classify each time-frequency bin into speech or\nnoise [2], [3]. In multichannel speech enhancement using phase\ninformation, the estimated masks are used for calculating the\nspatial covariance matrices (SCMs) of speech and noise. This\nallows one to use beamforming methods [4], [5]. Although this\napproach has successfully been used as a front end of ASR,\nthe performance of speech enhancement is often considerably\ndegraded in unseen noisy environments due to the nature of\nsupervised mask estimation [6].\nTo mitigate the sensitivity to acoustic characteristics of noisy\nenvironments, one may use unsupervised methods such as mul-\ntichannel extensions of nonnegative matrix factorization (NMF)\n[7]–[12]. Each variant of multichannel NMF (MNMF) can be\ninterpreted as maximum-likelihood or Bayesian estimation of a\nprobabilistic model representing the generative process of the\ncomplex spectrograms of mixture signals (e.g., speech + noise)\nand is used for general blind sound separation (BSS). The key\nassumption underlying the family of MNMF is that the power\nspectral densities (PSDs) of all sound sources have low-rank\nThis work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License. For more information, see https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
