      "text": "IEEE/ACM TRANSACTIONS ON AUDIO, SPEECH, AND LANGUAGE PROCESSING, VOL. 28, 2020\n1493\nA Joint Framework of Denoising Autoencoder\nand Generative Vocoder for Monaural\nSpeech Enhancement\nZhihao Du\n, Xueliang Zhang\n, Member, IEEE, and Jiqing Han\n, Member, IEEE\nAbstract—Conventional monaural speech enhancement meth-\nods usually enhance the magnitude spectrum of noisy speech and\nleave the phase unchanged. Recent studies suggest that phase is\nalsoimportantforbothspeechintelligibilityandperceptualquality.\nAlthough deep learning exhibits great potential on enhancing the\nmagnitude and phase spectra in complex spectrogram domain and\nwaveform domain, complex spectrogram and waveform are always\nmore difﬁcult to predict than the magnitude spectrum due to lack\nof clear structure in them. In this study, a Mel-domain denoising\nautoencoder and a deep generative vocoder are stacked to form a\njoint framework for monaural speech enhancement, in which the\nclean speech waveform is reconstructed without using the phase.\nSpeciﬁcally, a convolutional recurrent network (CRN) is employed\nas the denoising autoencoder to enhance the Mel power spectrum\nof noisy speech. Then, the enhanced Mel power spectrum is fed\nto a deep generative vocoder to synthesize the speech waveform.\nFurthermore, the denoising autoencoder and generative vocoder\nare jointly ﬁne-tuned. Experimental results show that the proposed\nmethod signiﬁcantly improves speech intelligibility and perceptual\nquality. More importantly, our method achieves much better gen-\neralization ability for untrained noises than previous methods.\nIndex Terms—Monaural speech enhancement, joint framework,\ndenoising autoencoder, generative vocoder.\nI. INTRODUCTION\nM\nONAURAL speech enhancement aims at separating\nspeech from non-speech interference or background\nnoise by using a single microphone. Traditional enhancement\nmethods ﬁrst decompose the noisy speech to magnitude and\nphase spectra through the short-time Fourier transform (STFT).\nThen, only the magnitude spectrum is enhanced while leaving\nthe phase spectrum unchanged. Finally, the enhanced magnitude\nspectrum and the phase spectrum of noisy speech are employed\ntosynthesizethespeechwaveformbyinverseSTFT[1].Inrecent\nManuscript received July 4, 2019; revised November 12, 2019, December 31,\n2019, February 5, 2020, and March 20, 2020; accepted April 27, 2020. Date of\npublication April 30, 2020; date of current version May 26, 2020. This work\nwas supported in part by National Natural Science Foundation of China under\nGrant U1736210, in part by National Key Research and Development Program\nof China under Grant 2017YFB1002102, and in part by National Natural Science\nFoundation of China under Grant 61876214. The associate editor coordinating\nthe review of this manuscript and approving it for publication was Dr. Andy W\nH Khong. (Corresponding author: Jiqing Han.)\nZhihao Du and Jiqing Han are with the School of Computer Science and\nTechnology, Harbin Institute of Technology, Harbin 150001, China (e-mail:\nduzhihao@hit.edu.cn; jqhan@hit.edu.cn).\nXueliang Zhang is with the Department of Computer Science, Inner Mongolia\nUniversity, Hohhot 010021, China (e-mail: cszxl@imu.edu.cn).\nDigital Object Identiﬁer 10.1109/TASLP.2020.2991537\nyears, monaural speech enhancement has been formulated as\na supervised learning problem and made tremendous progress\nbecause of the rapid development of deep learning [2]. As the\ntraditional enhancement methods, early deep learning based en-\nhancement methods also only enhance the magnitude spectrum\nand leave the noisy phase unchanged [3], such as the ideal binary\nmask (IBM) [4], ideal ratio mask (IRM) [5], phase-sensitive\nmask (PSM) [6] and spectral mapping [7]. However, recent\nstudies suggest that the phase is also crucial for both speech\nintelligibility and perceptual quality [8]. Only enhancing the\nmagnitude spectrum introduces speech distortion due to the\ninvalid STFT [9].\nRecently, many studies are making efforts on enhancing the\nmagnitude and phase spectra simultaneously by using the deep\nlearning based models. One method is transforming the mag-\nnitude and phase spectra from polar coordinate into Cartesian\ncoordinate by Euler’s formula to obtain the complex spectro-\ngram. Then, the magnitude and phase spectra are enhanced\nby estimating the complex ratio mask (CRM) [10]–[12] or\nclean counterparts [13]. Another method is directly performing\nenhancement in the time domain. For this purpose, the fully\nconvolutional network (FCN) [14] and Wavenet [15] are intro-\nduced to enhance the noisy waveform sample by sample. At\nthe same time, the UNet [16] is employed to predict the clean\nwaveform frame by frame, which is more efﬁcient. To train the\nUNet-like models, different loss functions are explored, such as\nthe adversarial loss in SEGAN [17] and the frequency domain\nloss in [18].\nWith the powerful modeling capacity, deep learning based\nmodels have shown the potential for enhancing complex spec-\ntrograms and waveforms. However, as discussed in [2], features\nand models play complementary roles in supervised learning.\nWhen the models have powerful modeling capacity, they place\nless demand on the features. On the contrary, when the features\nare more discriminative and with clear structures, they make the\nlearning task easier. Compared with the magnitude spectrum,\nthe complex spectrogram and waveform lack distinct structures\nmaking the learning task more difﬁcult. For example, the for-\nmant structure of speech is apparent in the magnitude spectrum,\nbutitisnotclearinthecomplexspectrogramandwaveform(seen\nin Fig. 1). Therefore, it would be better if we can only enhance\nthe magnitude spectrum of noisy speech and then synthesize the\nwaveform from the enhanced magnitude spectrum without using\nthe phase.\n2329-9290 © 2020 IEEE. Personal use is permitted, but republication/redistribution requires IEEE permission.\nSee https://www.ieee.org/publications/rights/index.html for more information.\nAuthorized licensed use limited to: Trial User - National Taiwan University. Downloaded on March 03,2026 at 08:11:15 UTC from IEEE Xplore.  Restrictions apply.",
