      "text": "1494\nIEEE/ACM TRANSACTIONS ON AUDIO, SPEECH, AND LANGUAGE PROCESSING, VOL. 28, 2020\nFig.1.\n(ColorOnline)Theexamplesof(a)waveform,(b)magnitudespectrum,\n(c)real,and(d)imaginarycomponentsinthecomplexspectrogramforthephrase\n“ﬁnd out.”\nIn the TTS community, a huge progress has been made on\ngenerativevocoderbyusingdeeplearningtechniques,whichcan\nsynthesize high-quality speech waveform from the Mel power\nspectrum in both the single-speaker [19]–[22] and multi-speaker\ntasks [23], [24]. Inspired by the progress in TTS, we stack a\nMel-domain denoising autoencoder (for enhancing the magni-\ntude spectrum) and a deep generative vocoder (for synthesizing\nthe waveform) to form a joint framework for monaural speech\nenhancement.\nIn the proposed method, a convolutional recurrent network\n(CRN) is trained to map the noisy Mel power spectrum to\nthe clean counterpart. Then, a normalizing ﬂow [25] based\ngenerative vocoder, Flowavenet [22], is employed to synthesize\nthe speech waveform from the enhanced Mel power spectrum.\nIn order to further improve the performance, the CRN and\nFlowavenet are stacked to build a deeper neural network and\njointly adjusted. Our contributions are three-fold.\nr We propose a joint framework for monaural speech en-\nhancement, in which the noisy Mel power spectrum is\nﬁrst enhanced and then consumed to synthesize the speech\nwaveform without using the phase.\nr We design a novel loss function to make the generative\nvocoder serve the speech enhancement purpose.\nr We develop a joint training method to improve speech\nintelligibility and perceptual quality further.\nExperimental results show that the proposed joint frame-\nwork signiﬁcantly improves speech intelligibility and perceptual\nquality for both trained and untrained noises.1 Moreover, the\n1Samples [Online]. Available: https://zhihaodu.github.io/dagv_demo/index.\nhtml\nproposed method has a much better generalization ability for\nuntrained noises than IRM [5], CRM [10], SEGAN [17], wave-\nform mapping [18], and the Wavenet for speech denoising [15].\nIn addition, the proposed joint training method further improves\nthe enhancement performance for both trained and untrained\nnoises.\nThe rest of this paper is organized as follows. Section II\nintroduces the normalizing ﬂow, Flowavenet, and the proposed\nloss function. Section III presents the details of the proposed\nframework and joint training method. In Section IV, we describe\nthe experiment setup. Section V provides the experimental re-\nsults and discussion. We concludes this study in Section VI.\nII. GENERATIVE VOCODER\nGenerative vocoders model the speech waveforms by using\nthe normalizing ﬂow technique [25], which can synthesize a\nspeech waveform from the clean Mel power spectrum and a\nrandom Gaussian noise [19]–[21]. Flowavenet is a generative\nvocoder based on deep neural networks, which is efﬁcient in\nboth the learning and generating processes [22].\nA. Normalizing Flow\nModelingthespeechwaveformwiththenormalizingﬂowisto\nﬁnd an invertible transformation function f(x) : x \u0002→z, which\nmaps the speech waveform x to a known prior z. Then, the log\nprobability of x can be directly calculated by using the change\nof variables [25], which is a basic technique in mathematics:\nlog Px(x) = log Pz(z) + log det\n\u0002∂f(x)\n∂x\n\u0003\n.\n(1)\nOnce a function f is invertible and its log determination of\nJacobian matrix (LDJ) is efﬁcient to calculate, we can apply a\nseries of K invertible functions to transform the speech wave-\nform x to a Gaussian noise z step by step (shortly called forward\npass in this study):\nz = f(x) = f(K) ◦f(K−1) ◦· · · ◦f(1)(x)\n(2)\nwhere ◦means composing two functions. The synthesized wave-\nform x′ can be obtained by sequentially applying the inverse\nfunctions on a Gaussian noise z (shortly called inverse pass in\nthis study):\nx′ = f −1(z) = f −1\n(1) ◦f −1\n(2) ◦· · · ◦f −1\n(K)(z).\n(3)\nThe LDJ of this composite function is given as follows:\nLDJf =\nK−1\n\u0004\nk=0\nlog det\n\u0005\n∂f(k+1)(x(k))\n∂x(k)\n\u0006\n(4)\nwhere x(0) is the speech waveform x. Note that there are no iter-\nations in the normalizing ﬂow based models. The log-likelihood\nof x and the synthesized waveform x′ are directly obtained from\nthe forward and inverse passes, respectively. In this study, we\ntrain the ﬂow-based generative vocoder to model the conditional\nprobability p(x| ¯S), where x and ¯S indicate the clean waveform\nand Mel power spectrum, respectively.\nAuthorized licensed use limited to: Trial User - National Taiwan University. Downloaded on March 03,2026 at 08:11:15 UTC from IEEE Xplore.  Restrictions apply.",
