      "text": "1502\nIEEE/ACM TRANSACTIONS ON AUDIO, SPEECH, AND LANGUAGE PROCESSING, VOL. 28, 2020\nFig. 6.\n(Color Online) The average (a) STOI and (b) PESQ scores of the\nproposed method and baselines over all evaluated SNR levels.\nFig. 7.\n(Color Online) The STOI and PESQ results of the Flowavenet and\nFlowavenet-our with the clean rescaled Mel power spectrum as a condition in\nﬁve times. The red and blue lines indicate the results of Flowavenet-our and\nFlowavenet, respectively. The solid and dashed lines represent the PESQ and\nSTOI score, respectively.\nof Flowavenet and Flowavenet-our indicate that the proposed\nloss function signiﬁcantly improves the STOI and PESQ. We\ncalculate the average result of ﬁve times and ﬁnd our method\nachieves 45.35% and 31.02% relative improvement for STOI\nand PESQ, respectively.\nWe also evaluate the impact of the proposed loss function\non the synthesis performance of WaveGlow [21], which has\na similar architecture and motivation as the Flowavenet. The\ndifference is that WaveGlow replaces the exchange order module\nin Flowavenet with the invertible 1 × 1 convolutions [26]. The\npre-trained WaveGlow provided in [21] is employed as the base-\nline, and the proposed loss function is used to further optimize\nTABLE V\nTHE AVERAGE STOI AND PESQ SCORES OF WAVEGLOW VOCODERS OVER\nTHE TRAINED AND UNTRAINED UTTERANCES\nthe pre-trained model, which is denoted as WaveGlow-prop.\nFor comparison, we also train the baseline by maximizing the\nlog-likelihood, which is denoted as WaveGlow-logp. The models\nare trained on a single GPU (TITAN Xp) with 12 GB memory,\nand the batch size is set to 2 due to memory limitation. Since the\nweights are pre-trained, the learning rate is set to a small number,\n1 × 10−6. The clean speech training set Strain is used for further\noptimizing, and the validation loss on Sval is calculated every\n2 k iterations. The models are trained for 40 k iterations, and the\nlowest validation losses of WaveGlow-logp and WaveGlow-prop\nare obtained at 8 k and 30 k iterations, respectively. Table V\nshows the STOI and PESQ scores of the further optimized\nmodels over 100 trained and 100 untrained utterances. From\nthe table, we can see that 8 k and 30 k iterations are large\nenough for WaveGlow-logp and WaveGlow-prop, respectively.\nTraining the models for more iterations does not provide a\nsigniﬁcant improvement in synthesis performance. Compared\nwith the baseline, further optimizing with the log-likelihood\ncannot signiﬁcantly improve the STOI and PESQ scores for both\ntrained and untrained utterances. This is because the baseline\nis pre-trained and converged for the log-likelihood. On the\ncontrary, the proposed loss function leads to about 0.40% STOI\nimprovement and 0.15 PESQ improvement for both trained and\nuntrained utterances. Hypothesis testing reveals that the im-\nprovement is signiﬁcant (p < 0.0001 for both STOI and PESQ\nscores). Therefore, the proposed loss function can improve the\nﬂow-based vocoders (Flowavenet and WaveGlow) in terms of\nSTOI and PESQ.\nIn the preliminary experiment, we tried to synthesize the clean\nwaveforms from the noisy Mel power spectra by using the clean-\ntrained vocoder, but it failed. This is because the distributions\nof noisy and clean features are quite different. We also tried\nto retrain the generative vocoder with the noisy speech signals.\nHowever, the distribution of noisy speeches has a much larger\nvariety range than that of clean ones, which cannot be modeled\nby the generative vocoder.\n3) Impact of Joint Training: We evaluate the impact of joint\ntraining for the framework IRM+Flowavenet-our. The results\nare given in the row IRM+Flowavenet-our+JT, Table IV. We\ncan see that the joint training improves IRM+Flowavenet-our\nwith respect to STOI and PESQ. There are two advantages\nof the joint training. First, it can reduce the mismatch be-\ntween the power spectrum enhanced by IRM and the desired\nMel power spectrum of Flowavenet. Second, the waveform\nAuthorized licensed use limited to: Trial User - National Taiwan University. Downloaded on March 03,2026 at 08:11:15 UTC from IEEE Xplore.  Restrictions apply.",
