      "text": "in which a deep Feed-forward Sequential Memory Network\n(DFSMN) with 10 DFSMN components is employed as the\nacoustic model. A pruned trigram English language model is\nused for decoding. The acoustic model is ﬁrst trained with the\nConnectionist Temporal Classiﬁcation (CTC) loss and then fur-\nther optimized by the state-level minimum Bayes risk (sMBR)\nbasedsequencediscriminativetraining.WetraintheASRsystem\nwith a large English corpus comprising about 15,000 hours data.\nNote that the used ASR system is a strong recognizer, which\nachieves about 9.73% word error rate (WER) on the clean test\nset.\nThe WER results of noisy and enhanced speech are shown\nin Fig. 8. From the ﬁgure, we can see that the proposed method\nsigniﬁcantly reduces the WERs at low SNR levels. For high SNR\nlevels (>10 dB), the enhanced speech still achieves lower WERs\nthan the noisy speech. This result indicates that our method\ndoes not introduce much speech distortion or artifact during the\nenhancement process.\nVI. CONCLUSION\nIn this study, we propose a joint framework for monaural\nspeech enhancement based on Mel power spectrum mapping and\ngenerative vocoder. We split the speech enhancement process\ninto two steps. The ﬁrst step is to enhance the Mel power spec-\ntrum, which is well structured and easy to predict. The second\nstep is to synthesize the speech waveform from the enhanced\nMel power spectrum. Experimental results show that, without\npredicting the clean phase or using the noisy phase, the proposed\nmethod signiﬁcantly improves speech intelligibility and percep-\ntual quality over the unprocessed mixture. More importantly,\nour method achieves much better noise generalization than pre-\nvious methods. In addition, the proposed method is time-causal,\nwhich is a demand for many real-world applications. Since the\ngenerative vocoder synthesizes speeches in a parallel way, it can\nmuch beneﬁt from the parallel computation ability of modern\ncomputers.\nAuthorized licensed use limited to: Trial User - National Taiwan University. Downloaded on March 03,2026 at 08:11:15 UTC from IEEE Xplore.  Restrictions apply. \n\n1504\nIEEE/ACM TRANSACTIONS ON AUDIO, SPEECH, AND LANGUAGE PROCESSING, VOL. 28, 2020\nAlthough we evaluate our method on a speaker-dependent\ndataset, the proposed method can be extended to speaker- inde-\npendent speech enhancement as well. It is because the generative\nvocoder can model hundreds of unique voices, and the mapping-\nbased enhancement methods can be speaker- independent as\nwell. In future studies, we will devote efforts to extending\nthe proposed method to speaker-independent application and\nevaluating the speaker generalization.\nACKNOWLEDGMENT\nWe would like to thank Sang-gil Lee for the discussion\nof Flowavenet and Shiliang Zhang for the help of evaluating\nthe speech distortion. We also would like to thank the anony-\nmous reviewers for their constructive comments. The work was\nperformed while the ﬁrst author was an internship at Elevoc\nTechnology Co., Ltd., ShenZhen, China.",
